>jjllambias@hotmail.com writes:
>Consider the text of each chapter of a book. Then the mass
>>of texts from all chapters are the text of the book, they
>>collaborate to be the text of the book.
>
>This is not obvious, albeit plausible.  How do the details go?
>
><How about a mass of many waters, can it be water?>
>
>Sure, that one seems to work, but I wonder if it isn't just malglico from 
>the mass-noun status of "water".

If you can treat water as a mass noun, then you can treat dog as a mass 
noun.  Western speakers are not used to doing so.  I think you end up with 
something akin to Mr. Dog (our Trobriand Island friend).

